Ablation studies. In Supplementary Figures [9](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.06226v2#A3.F9 "Supplementary Fig. 9 ‣ Appendix C Supplementary Figures ‣ A Versatile AI Agent for Rare Disease Diagnosis and Risk Gene Prioritization") (a) and (b), we show the contributions of adjusting the base models and the input information for these tasks. In both cases, changing the base models from GPT-4o to GPT-5 makes an obvious improvement, and incorporating more context, such as detailed phenotype descriptions, as well as having a verifier, can help Hygieia determine diseases and gene sets. We also find that providing disease information can help Hygieia rank genes; however, since in real clinical cases, physicians must know the results of the genetic tests and then make a diagnosis, we do not use this information as input for the prioritization of risk genes.

Human-in-the-loop design. We also consider the comparison and collaboration between physicians and AI models in making decisions. We have invited four genetic physicians from YSM, YNHH, and Duke-NUS Medical School, and consider two scenarios.

In our first experimental setting, we assign 23 questions (13 for disease diagnosis with 2 difficult questions related to extremely rare diseases, and 10 for gene prioritization) for X physicians and Hygieia, and evaluate the performances based on accuracy.

In the second experimental setting, we allow Hygieia to access the results provided by physicians, and utilize Hygieia for verifying the decision from physicians. If the verification turns out with negative results, we will run the correction pipeline discussed in our Methods and Results section to update the decision.

Case study investigation. To better demonstrate the advantages of Hygieia, we provide several case studies from different baseline methods and include the reasoning steps of Hygieia. In these questions, only Hygieia makes the correct decision, while the rest of the methods do not give us either correct reasoning paths or correct answers.

Explanations of baseline methods. For closed-source models, we consider LLMs with reasoning capacities including o4-mini [o3o4mini-systemcard-2025], GPT-5 [openai2025gpt5], and GPT-5 (search) [openai2025gpt5], advanced LLMs including GPT-4o [hurst2024gpt] and GPT-4o (search) [hurst2024gpt]. For open-source models, we consider Qwen3-8B [yang2025qwen3] and Deepseek-v3.1 [liu2024deepseek]. For domain-expert models, we consider a multi-agent diagnosis system (base model GPT-4o as the recommended setting), as well as DeepRare [zhao2025agentic]. However, DeepRare’s API version has bugs related to data processing, and we cannot find their released testing datasets, making it hard for us to retrieve the data format. For biomedical agents, we consider Biomni (base model GPT-4o as the recommended setting. For Qwen3-8B, we also consider fine-tuning the base model with both Low-rank adaptation (LoRA) and full parameters (Full) [zheng2024llamafactory] based on the provided simulation data. The prompts used to query LRM and LLMs are documented in Appendix [B](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.06226v2#A2 "Appendix B Prompts ‣ A Versatile AI Agent for Rare Disease Diagnosis and Risk Gene Prioritization"). For other models, details can be found in our code base.

Metrics. We follow the settings discussed in [chen2024rarebench, zhao2025agentic], where the Recall@@K is the main metric used in evaluating the generated disease diagnosis and gene rank results. We consider the top 1,5,10 candidates and compute Recall@@1, Recall@@5, Recall@@10, accordingly. We perform metric computation after disease name/gene name normalization.

